insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 1, 'Around the engines the course keeps three pieces of bookkeeping of its own. How does it time an event on a trace?', '["As the zero crossing nearest the stated time, searched over the 20 ms the bulk shift uses for its lags.", "As the sample of the largest amplitude within 8 ms of a stated time.", "As a time between samples, from a parabola fitted through the three largest samples near the stated time.", "As the first sample within 8 ms whose amplitude passes half of the trace''s own largest amplitude."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lab times an event as the sample of the largest amplitude within 8 ms of a stated time, so a pick always lands on the trace clock. It interpolates nothing between samples, and the 20 ms search belongs to the bulk shift.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 2, 'In this tier, what scale do the seismic amplitudes of the pack''s traces carry?', '["Reflection coefficients directly, because the kit''s seismic is SEG normal polarity and its top is a positive peak.", "Acoustic impedance in kg/(m2 s), the SI unit the engines work in for every velocity and density they are given.", "Decibels relative to the largest amplitude on each trace, so every trace peaks at exactly 0 and reads below it.", "None: they are the kit''s own trace units, tied to a reflection coefficient only by a scale."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Seismic amplitudes stay in the kit''s trace units, which carry no physical scale, so a reflection coefficient and an amplitude meet only through a scale the last module measures. SEG normal polarity fixes the sign of a peak and says nothing about its size.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 3, 'In what unit does synthetics.js compute the impedance it turns into reflection coefficients?', '["In (m/s)(g/cc), the AI / 1000 form, so the Ekene-1 oil-leg mean reads 6639.018603.", "In kg/(m2 s), where the Ekene-1 oil-leg mean reads 6639018.603084.", "In units of 10^6 kg/(m2 s), where the same mean reads 6.639019.", "In m/s alone, since the density log enters only the cap rock."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s convention is impedance in (m/s)(g/cc), which is AI in kg/(m2 s) divided by 1000: 6639.018603 for the Ekene-1 oil-leg mean. The kg/(m2 s) and 10^6 forms print the same quantity in units the synthetic engine does not use.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 4, 'A log sample is a LAS null. What does the synthetic engine do with it?', '["It interpolates the impedance linearly across the null from the live samples above and below, so the synthetic runs unbroken.", "It refuses the whole log, since a convolution needs a finite reflection coefficient at every sample it is given and a LAS null has none.", "It treats the sample as a gap: the convolution zero-fills it, and a convolved value whose own coefficient is a gap is not used.", "It keeps the convolved value at that sample, since the zero fill has made the coefficient there a finite number."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A sample that is not finite or is a seismic or LAS null is a gap; the convolution zero-fills it and discards the convolved value at the gap itself. So the Ekene-1 synthetic is live on 26 samples only, where the logs are, and nothing is interpolated or refused.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 5, 'On Ekene-1''s full-stack trace clock of 4.000000 ms, how many live synthetic samples do the pack''s logs give?', '["31, one for every sample of the statistical wavelet the synthetic is convolved with on the same clock.", "800, one for every log sample, since the log step of 0.500000 ft is finer than the seismic clock.", "26, because the logs span only 4931.500000 to 5331.000000 ft.", "12, the number of CDP gathers next to Ekene-1, since each gather supplies one live synthetic sample."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pack''s logs cover the Ekene Sand and the shale above it, 4931.500000 to 5331.000000 ft, which resamples to 26 live samples on the 4.000000 ms clock. The 800 log samples collapse onto that clock, and the 31 samples belong to the wavelet.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 6, 'What is the largest reflection coefficient in the live span of the Ekene-1 synthetic, and where is it?', '["-0.190340, at 1312.000000 ms.", "-0.190340 at 1311.086000 ms", "-0.190340 at 1288.796378 ms", "-0.105222 at 1312.000000 ms"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The largest coefficient is -0.190340 at 1312.000000 ms. Under SEG normal polarity an increase in impedance is positive, so this negative one is a fall in impedance, below the top of the sand at 1288.796378 ms; 1311.086000 ms is a checkshot time and -0.105222 a correlation from the designed phase case.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 7, 'estimatePhaseRotation is run on the Ekene-1 synthetic against its full-stack trace. What does it report?', '["A best phase of -36.092689 degrees, the correlation rising from 0.799189 to 0.989219.", "A best phase of -96.113046 degrees, the correlation rising to 0.988309.", "A best phase of -6.135261 degrees, the correlation held at 0.983438 unchanged.", "A best phase of -6.135261 degrees, the correlation rising from 0.983438 to 0.989111."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At Ekene-1 the best phase is -6.135261 degrees and the correlation goes from 0.983438 before rotation to 0.989111 after, a small gain. The -36.092689 and -96.113046 rows are the designed case with 30 and 90 degrees added first.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 8, 'Of the two wavelets the seismic can give at a well, which one has a MEASURED phase?', '["The statistical wavelet, because the autocorrelation of the traces keeps the phase of the source wavelet intact.", "The least-squares (Wiener) wavelet that best convolves the well''s reflectivity into the trace at the well.", "The 25 Hz Ricker on the same clock, because its phase is set by its formula and so needs no assumption.", "Neither: both are taken to zero phase, and a phase is only ever measured later by the bulk shift search."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The wavelet from the well is the Wiener wavelet fitted to carry the well''s reflectivity into the trace, so its phase is measured. The statistical wavelet comes from the autocorrelation''s amplitude spectrum taken to zero phase, so its phase is assumed zero.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 9, 'What stated inputs does extractStatisticalWavelet read in the course''s ties, and what does it do to the lags?', '["A 20 ms window and 8 Hz smoothing, the bulk shift''s search and the pick''s reach; it tapers nothing.", "A 120 ms window and no smoothing, with the lags left untapered.", "A 1100 to 1500 ms window with 5 Hz smoothing; it scales the wavelet to unit energy in place of a peak.", "A 120 ms window and 5 Hz smoothing; it Hann-tapers the lags and normalises the peak to 1."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine reads a window length of 120 ms and a smoothing of 5 Hz, Hann-tapers the lags and normalises the peak to 1. The 1100 to 1500 ms span is the trace window, a separate choice, and unit energy is the wavelet comparison''s scaling.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 10, 'On the pack''s logs, at what half length does the wavelet from the well at Ekene-1 come back as a wavelet, with damping 0.001?', '["At 15 samples, its default: a 31-sample wavelet from the 26 live samples of the synthetic, with a measured phase.", "At 3 samples: a 7-sample wavelet from 20 samples, fit correlation 0.946989.", "At 5 samples: an 11-sample wavelet from the 16 usable samples the log covers at the well, the engine''s own count.", "At none of them: the well reflectivity is flat at every half length, so only the statistical wavelet ever comes back."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the default half length of 15 the engine says "The well reflectivity is flat over the seismic at the well, so there is nothing to extract a wavelet from." At 5 it counts 16 usable samples against the 22 an 11-sample wavelet needs and refuses; at 3 it returns a 7-sample wavelet from 20 samples, fit correlation 0.946989.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 11, 'Set the 25 Hz Ricker beside the Ekene-1 statistical wavelet on the same 31-sample clock. What does the course''s table show?', '["The Ricker peaks at 25.039790 Hz with its centroid at 26.596139 Hz; Ekene-1 peaks at 24.488746 Hz with its centroid at 25.084884 Hz.", "The two wavelets match: the Ricker peaks at 25.039790 Hz and Ekene-1 centres at 25.084884 Hz, so either serves.", "The Ricker''s centroid equals its 25 Hz peak, since a Ricker is symmetric and its power sits about one frequency.", "Ekene-1''s centroid, 26.596139 Hz, sits above the Ricker''s 25.084884 Hz, because a field wavelet carries more high frequency."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Ricker peaks at 25.039790 Hz and has its centroid at 26.596139 Hz, while Ekene-1 peaks at 24.488746 Hz with its centroid at 25.084884 Hz. The two wavelets do not match figure for figure, and the swapped figures belong to the other wavelet.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 12, 'Which well''s statistical wavelet has the highest -6 dB high edge, and what is it?', '["Ekene-3, 42.236734 Hz.", "Ekene-2, 39.582002 Hz.", "Ekene-4, at 43.780487 Hz.", "Ekene-1, 41.469972 Hz."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-4 is highest in every column: peak 26.530951 Hz, centroid 26.581599 Hz and high edge 43.780487 Hz. Ekene-3''s 42.236734 Hz is second, Ekene-1''s 41.469972 Hz third, and Ekene-2''s 39.582002 Hz is the lowest.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 13, 'In the designed case the Ekene-1 synthetic is first turned by 90 degrees and then matched to the trace again. What does the match return?', '["Best phase -66.081556 degrees; correlation 0.400777 before and 0.988816 after, the same rotation found back.", "Best phase -96.113046 degrees; correlation -0.105222 before and 0.988309 after.", "A refusal, since the correlation before rotation is negative.", "Best phase -96.113046 degrees; correlation stuck at -0.105222, since one rotation cannot repair a reversed match."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With 90 degrees added the correlation before matching is -0.105222, and the match finds -96.113046 degrees and restores it to 0.988309. The -66.081556 row belongs to 60 degrees added, and the engine returns a phase for any correlation.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 14, 'Why does the lab run estimatePhaseRotation on the synthetic''s live span only?', '["Because the trace outside 1100 to 1500 ms went into the statistical wavelet and must stay apart from the match.", "Because the 20 ms bulk shift must fit inside it.", "Because the zero fill would fix the phase.", "Because the Hilbert transform cannot cross a gap."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The best phase is found over the samples both traces carry, and the lab restricts it to the synthetic''s live span, the 26 samples at Ekene-1, because the Hilbert transform cannot cross a gap. The wavelet window and the bulk shift search do not set this span.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-feasibility-to-the-tie', 15, 'What does rotateConstantPhase return for a trace s turned through a phase phi?', '["s moved later in time by a lag set by phi, the way the bulk shift moves a synthetic.", "H(s) cos(phi) + s sin(phi), so that a phase of zero returns the Hilbert transform.", "s cos(phi) + H(s) sin(phi), with H the Hilbert transform.", "s cos(phi) alone, a pure scaling of amplitude."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rotation is s cos(phi) + H(s) sin(phi): a phase of zero leaves s as it is and 180 degrees gives the reversed polarity trace. A time shift is the bulk shift''s job, and swapping s and H(s) would return the Hilbert transform at zero phase.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 1, 'suggestBulkShift returns a POSITIVE shift for a well. What does the sign mean?', '["The seismic event is earlier, so the synthetic moves up the trace to meet it.", "The seismic event is later, so the synthetic moves down.", "The checkshots read too late by the shift.", "The polarity is reversed at that well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A positive bulk shift means the seismic event is later, so the synthetic moves down. The designed check shows it: checkshot times moved by -8.000000 ms come back as a shift of 8.000000 ms. The checkshots themselves stay as imported, and polarity is the phase match''s question.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 2, 'Which pack wells need a non-zero bulk shift, each with the statistical wavelet of its own trace?', '["Ekene-1 and Ekene-3, each by -4.000000 ms, the two wells whose wavelets are the most alike of the four.", "None: all four tie at 0.000000 ms.", "Ekene-2 and Ekene-4, each by 8.000000 ms, the move the designed check plants in the checkshot times.", "Ekene-2 and Ekene-4, each by -4.000000 ms."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 tie best at -4.000000 ms, with correlations 0.965477 and 0.962720, while Ekene-1 and Ekene-3 need 0.000000 ms. The 8.000000 ms figure belongs to the designed check.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 3, 'Over what lags does suggestBulkShift search for the largest correlation of the synthetic with the trace?', '["Every whole-sample lag up to the stated search of 20 ms, on the samples both traces carry.", "Every lag up to 5 samples, the maximum lag compareWavelets allows in its alignment.", "Fractional lags at 1 ms steps up to 8 ms, the reach of the event pick.", "Lags up to 30 degrees of phase, the span the issue register flags."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bulk shift takes the normalised cross-correlation at every whole-sample lag up to the stated 20 ms search, on the samples both carry, and keeps the lag of the largest. So Ekene-2''s -4.000000 ms is one sample on the 4.000000 ms clock; 5 samples is the wavelet comparison''s lag and 8 ms the pick''s reach.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 4, 'Ekene-1''s checkshot times are moved by -8.000000 ms before the synthetic is built. What does the bulk shift find?', '["-8.000000 ms, the same sign as the planted move, with a correlation of 0.983438.", "8.000000 ms, with the correlation dropping to 0.965477 because the synthetic has moved.", "8.000000 ms, with a correlation of 0.983438.", "0.000000 ms, since the stated search of 20 ms is measured from the moved checkshot times."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The search returns the opposite of the planted move, 8.000000 ms, at a correlation of 0.983438, the same figure the unshifted tie prints. 0.965477 is Ekene-2''s tie.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 5, 'A tie carries anchors at (1250, 1252) and (1350, 1356) ms. To what seismic time does the synthetic time 1400.000000 ms map?', '["1406.000000 ms: beyond the last anchor the mapping continues with slope one.", "1356.000000 ms: past the last anchor every time is held there.", "1304.000000 ms: halfway between the anchors, half of each shift.", "1202.000000 ms: past the anchors the warp falls back to the first anchor''s shift, as one anchor would give."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Between anchors the mapping is linear, and beyond the first and the last it continues with slope one, a constant shift; so 1400.000000 ms maps to 1406.000000 ms with the last anchor''s shift. 1304.000000 ms is where 1300.000000 ms maps, and 1202.000000 ms is where 1200.000000 ms maps.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 6, 'What does makeTieWarp do with a single anchor?', '["It refuses, since a linear mapping between anchors needs at least two of them to define a slope.", "It stretches the whole trace about the anchor so that every time scales by the ratio of its two times.", "It applies the anchor''s shift at that time only and leaves every other sample of the synthetic in place.", "It applies one constant shift: a bulk shift."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Beyond the first and the last anchor the mapping continues with slope one, a constant shift, so the single-anchor case is a bulk shift. Nothing is stretched about the anchor and nothing is refused.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 7, 'A learner gives makeTieWarp a second anchor whose seismic time is earlier than the first anchor''s. What happens?', '["It sorts the anchors by seismic time and builds the warp from the reordered pair without any comment.", "It refuses: both times must increase from one anchor to the next, and this pair would fold time.", "It drops the second anchor and keeps a bulk shift at the first.", "It maps the interval with a negative slope, running time backwards."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both times must increase from one anchor to the next, and makeTieWarp refuses a pair that would fold time. Its message carries a punctuation mark the course does not print, so the course names it by what it refuses.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 8, 'A tie is accepted at a well. What happens to the time-depth record?', '["The imported checkshots are overwritten by the warped times, so the record holds one set, the tied one.", "Only the bulk shift is stored, as one number added to every checkshot''s one-way time.", "Rows derived from its anchors are kept as a new set beside the imported checkshots, which stay as imported.", "The record is held inside QI Studio alone, and Seismolord keeps its own separate untied checkshots."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Once a tie is accepted, rows derived from its anchors through the same time-depth line are kept as a new set beside the imported checkshots, which stay as they were imported. QI Studio reads the tie each well has in Seismolord, with its QC record, so every app reads one tie.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 9, 'At Ekene-1, what is the two-way time of the top of the Ekene Sand, 5078.740157 ft, linear between checkshot levels?', '["1288.876000 ms, the two-way time of the checkshot level at 5079.002625 ft, the nearest one to the top.", "644.398189 ms, the one-way time the checkshots give at the top, read as two-way time without doubling.", "1311.086000 ms, the two-way time of the checkshot at 5183.989501 ft, the level next to the base.", "1288.796378 ms."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Linear between the levels at 4687.500000 ft and 5079.002625 ft, the top lands at 1288.796378 ms, just above that level''s 1288.876000 ms. Two-way time is 2 x the one-way time, so 644.398189 ms is the one-way figure, and 1311.086000 ms sits by the base.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 10, 'A well ties with a non-zero bulk shift. What does the shift do to the seismic times of the depths on its tie?', '["It adds the shift to the seismic time of every depth, the top''s included, and leaves the imported checkshot rows as they were.", "Nothing: the committed seismic time of every depth stays its imported checkshot time, whatever bulk shift the tie found at the well.", "It moves only the top of the reservoir, the one event the tie was matched on.", "It scales each in proportion to depth."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A bulk shift is the single-anchor warp: it adds the shift to the seismic time of every depth on the tie, the top''s included, and leaves the imported checkshot rows as they were. At Ekene-1 the bulk shift is zero, so the top''s committed time is its checkshot time; Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 tied at -4.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 11, 'How does compareWavelets measure the similarity of two wavelets?', '["As the ratio of their peak frequencies, so Ekene-2''s 23.394907 Hz against Ekene-4''s 26.530951 Hz sets the pair''s figure.", "After aligning by the lag of largest correlation, up to 5 samples, and scaling to unit energy, as the zero-lag correlation.", "As the correlation at zero lag on the raw wavelets, so a stronger trace''s wavelet scores higher against the reference.", "As the correlation at the best lag within the 20 ms bulk shift search, with every wavelet normalised to a peak of 1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first wavelet is the reference; every other is aligned to it by the lag of largest correlation, up to 5 samples, all are scaled to unit energy, and the zero-lag correlation of each pair is the similarity. Unit energy removes strength, so a stronger trace scores no higher.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 12, 'Which two pack wells have the least alike statistical wavelets, and at what similarity?', '["Ekene-1 and Ekene-3, 0.999524.", "Ekene-2 and Ekene-3, at 0.994059, the lowest Ekene-3 pair.", "Ekene-2 and Ekene-4, at 0.986771.", "Ekene-1 and Ekene-4, at 0.996130, the reference against the farthest well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The smallest similarity in the matrix is Ekene-2 with Ekene-4, 0.986771, the lowest and highest frequency wells. Ekene-1 with Ekene-3, 0.999524, is the most alike pair, and every lag to the reference is 0 samples.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 13, 'compareWavelets averages the four Ekene statistical wavelets into one field wavelet. What does the average carry?', '["61 samples at 2 ms, peak 24.769590 Hz.", "31 samples, peak 25.039790 Hz, a 25 Hz Ricker.", "31 samples, peak 24.488746 Hz, centroid 25.084884 Hz, since the reference well''s spectrum is kept as it is.", "31 samples, peak 24.769590 Hz, centroid 25.155328 Hz."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The average is the mean of the aligned, unit-energy wavelets, scaled to unit energy again: 31 samples, peak 24.769590 Hz, centroid 25.155328 Hz. 61 samples is the average resampled from 4 ms to 2 ms; the Ricker and Ekene-1 rows are other wavelets.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 14, 'Ekene-3''s wavelet is rotated before the comparison, once by 90 degrees and once by 180 degrees. Which case breaks the 0.8 rule for a well unlike the others?', '["Only the reversed polarity case: Ekene-3 at 0.693702; at 90 degrees it stays above the rule at 0.911984.", "Both: at 90 degrees Ekene-3 falls to 0.911984 and reversed to 0.693702, each under the 0.8 rule.", "Neither: the other three wells drop with it, and the average absorbs a single well''s rotation.", "Only the 90 degree case, since a quarter-turn spans more than the register''s 30 degrees."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reversed, Ekene-3 correlates with the average at 0.693702, below 0.8; at 90 degrees it scores 0.911984 and passes. The 30 degree span is a separate phase rule, and in both cases the pulled average lowers the other wells too.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-tie-and-the-field-wavelet', 15, 'Under QI Studio''s issue register rules, how is a tie with a mean windowed correlation of 0.6 graded?', '["Poor, below the 0.7 rule.", "Unlike the others, since a correlation below the 0.8 rule sends the well out of the field wavelet.", "Fair: below 0.7, at or above 0.5.", "Flagged, since a tie correlation below 0.7 is treated like a phase span of more than 30 degrees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A tie whose mean windowed correlation is below 0.5 is poor and one below 0.7 fair, so 0.6 is fair. The 0.8 rule applies to a wavelet''s correlation with the average, and the flag applies to phases spanning more than 30 degrees.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 1, 'Which of the pack''s 12 CDP gathers carries the trace of Ekene-1?', '["Gather 0, inline 1026, crossline 2050.", "Gather 9, at inline 1028, crossline 2051, the gather with the same crossline on the last inline of the three.", "Gather 5, at inline 1027, crossline 2051.", "Gather 6, at inline 1027, crossline 2052, the gather beside it on the same inline as the well''s trace."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gather 5 is at inline 1027, crossline 2051, the trace of Ekene-1, among 12 gathers over inlines 1026 to 1028. Gathers 6 and 9 are its neighbours, and gather 0 is the first corner of the set.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 2, 'binOffsets puts the 13 offsets of a pack gather into bins of the stated width of 200 m. What does foldSummary report?', '["13 traces, 13 live bins, far offset 2500.000000 m.", "13 traces, 12 live bins, since the 100 m offset and the next one share the first bin.", "13 traces, 13 live bins, far offset 2400 m.", "13 traces, 7 live bins of fold 2."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 200 m bin k holds offsets from k w to (k + 1) w, and the 13 offsets from 100 to 2500 m fill 13 bins with fold 1 each. A bin is reported at its centre, (k + 0.5) w, so the far offset reads 2500.000000 m, the farthest recorded offset.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 3, 'At what offset does foldSummary report a bin of width w?', '["At its near edge, k w, the smallest offset the bin could hold.", "At the mean of the offsets that actually fall in it.", "At its far edge, (k + 1) w, so the far offset never understates the reach of the gather.", "At its centre, (k + 0.5) w."]'::jsonb, 3, 'foldSummary reports a bin at its centre, (k + 0.5) w, whatever offsets landed in it. With w at 200 m, the bin of the 2500 m trace is centred on that trace, which is why the summary''s far offset matches the recording.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 4, 'The top of the Ekene Sand is picked on the nearest offset of gather 5 about 1289 ms. Where does the pick land, and why there?', '["1288.796378 ms, the checkshot time, returned unchanged.", "1292.000000 ms, a sample on the gather''s 4.000000 ms clock.", "1312.000000 ms, the time of the synthetic''s largest reflection coefficient, since the pick follows the strongest event in the span.", "1289 ms exactly, interpolated between samples."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An event''s time on a trace is the sample of the largest amplitude within 8 ms of a stated time, so the pick lands on a sample of the 4.000000 ms clock, 1292.000000 ms. 1288.796378 ms is the checkshot time, which lies between samples, and 1312.000000 ms is outside the 8 ms reach.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 5, 'How does nmoStretch measure the stretch of an event at offset x and zero-offset time t0?', '["As t / t0 - 1, with t = sqrt(t0^2 + x^2 / v^2) and v the NMO velocity.", "As t - t0 in milliseconds, the moveout itself.", "As x / (v t0), growing in a straight line with offset.", "As the fall of the centroid from near to far traces."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The stretch is t / t0 - 1, with t = sqrt(t0^2 + x^2 / v^2) and v the NMO velocity, so it is a fraction. On gather 5 it is 0.000525 at 100 m and 0.286880 at 2500 m.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 6, 'On gather 5 at the top, t0 1292.000000 ms, what NMO velocity does the course use for the stretch?', '["The interval velocity of the Ekene Sand, since the stretch happens inside the reservoir layer itself.", "The Ekene-1 oil-leg mean Vp, 2865.750738 m/s, read from the logs at the top.", "The best AB semblance velocity there.", "The table''s RMS velocity there, 2388.940541 m/s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At the top on gather 5 the NMO velocity is the RMS velocity of the pack''s table at 1292.000000 ms, 2388.940541 m/s. The log Vp of 2865.750738 m/s is a property of the sand, and semblance is how residual moveout measures, a separate step.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 7, 'NMO correction draws the top event out at the far traces. What stretch does nmoStretch give at 2500 m on Ekene-1''s gather?', '["0.209511, the figure at 2100 m, the next offset in, read as the far trace of the gather.", "0.000525, the stretch at 100 m, since the stretch is read at the nearest offset for the gather.", "0.286880.", "0.300000, the limit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stretch at 2500 m is 0.286880, against 0.000525 at 100 m and 0.209511 at 2100 m. 0.300000 is a stated limit and no measured stretch.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 8, 'At the top of gather 5, what offset does stretchMuteOffset return for a stated limit of 0.300000?', '["2047.359898 m, the mute offset the same formula gives for a limit of 0.200000 at that time.", "2563.848741 m, from v t0 sqrt((1 + limit)^2 - 1).", "2500.000000 m, since the engine caps the mute offset at the farthest offset the gather records.", "2388.940541 m, the velocity read as an offset."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The stretch mute is the offset at which the stretch reaches the stated limit, v t0 sqrt((1 + limit)^2 - 1): 2563.848741 m at 0.300000 and 2047.359898 m at 0.200000. The engine returns the offset the formula gives, beyond the 2500 m far offset here, and 2388.940541 m/s is the velocity.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 9, 'Which offsets of a gather enter residualMoveout''s fit dt = a x^2?', '["Those at least 0.7 and positive, since a negative match is a reversed event.", "Every offset equally, near and far traces weighing the same.", "Those at least 0.5, the register''s floor for a poor tie.", "Those whose correlation with the pilot is at least 0.7 in size, weighted by the squared correlation."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Offsets whose correlation is at least 0.7 in size enter the fit, weighted by the squared correlation. The size counts, so on gather 5 the last three offsets, at correlations -0.776230, -0.728225 and -0.786646, are among the 13 fitted.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 10, 'On gather 5, the last three offsets give lags of -24.000000 ms against the pilot. What does that figure mean?', '["They sit at the 24 ms largest shift, the search limit, with correlations of -0.776230, -0.728225 and -0.786646.", "They are the far-offset residual moveout, read as 24 ms of uncorrected curvature.", "They are the bulk shift the tie found at Ekene-1, carried onto the gather as a static on the far traces.", "They are a refusal: a lag at the search limit drops the offset from the fit, leaving 10 offsets fitted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The lag search runs to a largest shift of 24 ms, and the last three offsets land on that limit with negative correlations. All 13 offsets are fitted, and the far-offset residual the engine returns is 2.795940 ms; Ekene-1''s bulk shift is 0.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 11, 'On what condition does residualMoveout measure by AB semblance, as it does on gather 5?', '["When any offset''s lag reaches the 24 ms largest shift, since a lag at the limit is no measurement of the event.", "When the far-offset residual exceeds the 4.000000 ms sample interval, the size at which a lag fit stops resolving it.", "When the fitted lags scatter about the parabola by more than one sample, so the waveform has changed with offset.", "When fewer than 3 offsets correlate with the pilot at 0.7 or more, too few to fit the curvature a x^2 at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'When the fitted lags scatter about the parabola by more than one sample the waveform has changed with offset, and the engine measures by AB semblance. On gather 5 that is the case with all 13 offsets in the fit, and its residual of 2.795940 ms is under one sample.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 12, 'Across the 12 pack gathers, which has the largest far-offset residual moveout?', '["Gather 5, at 2.795940 ms, the gather at Ekene-1, where the well''s tie fixes the event most firmly.", "Gather 3, at 3.002917 ms.", "Gather 10, 2.953658 ms.", "Gather 0, at 2.939718 ms, the corner gather of the set at inline 1026, crossline 2050."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The residuals run from 2.795940 ms at gather 5, the smallest, to 3.002917 ms at gather 3, the largest; every gather picks its top at 1292.000000 ms and measures by ab-semblance. Gathers 10 and 0 lie between.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 13, 'Mapped from offset to angle at the picked top of Ekene-1''s gather, how many traces fall in each of its three 10 degree angle bins, near to far?', '["1, 1 and 1, the fold of every 200 m offset bin carried across unchanged.", "3, 3 and 2, since the near bin holds the 3 traces stacked for the pilot.", "4, 4 and 5, the 13 offsets spread over three bins with none lost to the mute.", "2, 3 and 2."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mapped to angle at 1292.000000 ms, the bins 5 to 15, 15 to 25 and 25 to 35 degrees hold fold 2, 3 and 2. In offset at 200 m every bin held fold 1, and the 3 pilot traces belong to residual moveout.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 14, 'A study states a stretch limit of 0.200000 at the picked top of Ekene-1''s gather. Which tabled offsets carry a stretch above that limit?', '["2100 m and 2500 m, at 0.209511 and 0.286880.", "2500 m alone, at 0.286880.", "1700 m and beyond, from 0.141649.", "None: the mute lies past 2500 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 0.200000 the stretch mute offset is 2047.359898 m, and the tabled stretch passes the limit at 2100 m (0.209511) and 2500 m (0.286880); 1700 m sits under it at 0.141649. The stated limit decides which far offsets a study keeps, and the far offsets carry the largest angles.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-gathers-and-their-qc', 15, 'What mute do the pack''s gathers carry?', '["A stretch mute at the 0.200000 limit, applied by stretchMuteOffset at every gather.", "The kit''s stated mute beyond 40 degrees, by the kit''s own angle calculation, which the course does not measure.", "A mute beyond 35.000000 degrees, the usable angle at a minimum fold of 2 on gather 5, written into the gathers so that no trace beyond it carries a sample.", "No mute: the 13 offsets from 100 to 2500 m are all live at every time from 1000 to 1700 ms."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gathers carry the kit''s stated mute beyond 40 degrees, by the kit''s own angle calculation, which the course does not measure. The stretch mute and the usable angle are figures a study computes and states; neither is written into the pack''s gathers.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 1, 'In the pack''s RMS velocity table, what Dix interval velocity does dixInterval return for the interval that closes at the row of 1311.000000 ms?', '["2396.900000 m/s, the RMS velocity printed on that row.", "2077.601943 m/s, the interval from 1040.500000 to 1288.800000 ms.", "2885.878392 m/s, the interval that starts at the row of 1288.800000 ms.", "2388.940541 m/s, the RMS velocity the trace clock puts at 1292.000000 ms between the two bracketing rows."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dix takes the RMS velocities and times of two rows and returns 2885.878392 m/s for the interval that closes at 1311.000000 ms. 2396.900000 is that row''s RMS velocity, 2077.601943 belongs to the interval above, and 2388.940541 is an RMS velocity on the trace clock.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 2, 'A designed RMS velocity table whose RMS velocity falls too fast is passed to dixInterval. What comes back?', '["A refusal in the engine''s own words: \"The RMS velocities give no real interval velocity at 1.1 s.\"", "An interval velocity of zero at 1.1 s, the negative square clamped up to nothing.", "The deeper row''s RMS velocity, the rule the engine keeps for a table''s first row.", "A NaN interval velocity at 1.1 s, a result with an empty field and no refusal."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A pair of rows whose numerator Vrms2^2 t2 - Vrms1^2 t1 is negative has no real interval velocity, and the engine refuses, naming the time: "The RMS velocities give no real interval velocity at 1.1 s." Only the first row takes its RMS velocity as its interval velocity.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 3, 'velocityOnGrid puts the pack''s table on the trace clock. What interval velocity does it give the sample at 1292.000000 ms, and from what?', '["2077.601943 m/s, printed on the last row above the sample.", "2388.940541 m/s, the RMS velocity reused as the interval velocity.", "A value linear in time between 2077.601943 and 2885.878392 m/s, read the same way as the RMS velocity column.", "2885.878392 m/s, the Dix velocity of the row segment the sample falls in."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the trace clock the RMS velocity is linear in time between rows (2388.940541 m/s at 1292.000000 ms), and the interval velocity is the Dix velocity of the segment the sample falls in, from 1288.800000 to 1311.000000 ms: 2885.878392 m/s. It steps at each row.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 4, 'A sample lies later than the last row of the pack''s RMS table, 1782.300000 ms. What RMS velocity does velocityOnGrid give it?', '["2132.930307 m/s, the last segment''s Dix interval velocity carried on.", "2328.600000 m/s, the last row''s RMS velocity, held flat outside the table.", "A refusal naming the time, as the sample lies outside the table.", "A value extrapolated along the line through the last two rows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The RMS velocity is linear between rows and held flat outside the table, so a sample past 1782.300000 ms reads 2328.600000 m/s. 2132.930307 is an interval velocity, and the engine neither refuses nor extrapolates.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 5, 'On the Ekene table at the top of gather 5 (1292.000000 ms), what is Walden''s angle for the 1500 m offset?', '["28.884086 degrees, Walden''s angle at 1500 m in the designed four-layer earth, read at the base of its third layer.", "28.628580 degrees, the exact ray-trace angle at 1500 m through the designed four-layer teaching earth.", "31.872141 degrees.", "41.065011 degrees, Walden''s angle on the Ekene table at the next tabled offset, the 2000 m trace."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With Vrms 2388.940541 and Vint 2885.878392 m/s at 1292.000000 ms, Walden''s angle at 1500 m is 31.872141 degrees. 28.884086 and 28.628580 belong to the teaching earth at 1596.736597 ms, and 41.065011 is the 2000 m offset.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 6, 'The course compares Walden''s angle with an exact ray trace at the base of the third layer of the designed teaching earth. What does the comparison show?', '["Walden reads low at every offset, by 0.009482 degrees at 500 m and 2.143321 at 3000 m.", "Walden and the ray trace differ by a near constant 0.255506 degrees at every offset.", "Walden reads high by 2.143321 degrees at 500 m, shrinking to 0.009482 at 3000 m.", "Walden reads high at every tabled offset, by 0.009482 degrees at 500 m and by 2.143321 at 3000 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Walden less ray trace is positive in every row and grows with offset: 0.009482 degrees at 500 m, 0.255506 at 1500 m, 2.143321 at 3000 m. At 500 m the excess is under a hundredth of a degree; at 3000 m it passes two degrees.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 7, 'waldenAngle is given an offset so large that its sine works out above one. What does it return?', '["90 degrees.", "A refusal naming the offset, the way dixInterval refuses a negative numerator and names its time.", "A NaN angle, a result with an empty field that the angle gather then skips like a seismic null sample.", "40 degrees, the angle of the kit''s mute, since every trace past it carries exact zeros at the target."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Past a sine of one waldenAngle returns 90 degrees: it neither refuses nor returns NaN. The kit''s mute beyond 40 degrees is a property of the gathers, and the engine knows nothing of it.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 8, 'The pack''s gathers carry the kit''s mute beyond 40 degrees. On the Ekene table at 1292.000000 ms, between which tabled offsets does Walden''s angle pass 40 degrees?', '["Between 2000 m (41.065011 degrees) and 2500 m (49.493998 degrees), so only the 2500 m trace reaches the mute zone.", "Between 1500 m (31.872141 degrees) and 2000 m (41.065011 degrees).", "Between 1000 m (21.859471 degrees) and 1500 m.", "Beyond 2500 m, the far offset staying inside the mute."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Walden''s angle at the top is 31.872141 degrees at 1500 m and 41.065011 at 2000 m, so it passes 40 degrees between them. At 2500 m it is 49.493998 degrees. The kit''s mute comes from the kit''s own angle calculation, which the course does not measure.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 9, 'How does angleGather treat the mute''s exact zeros at a sample?', '["As gaps, skipped like a seismic null sample.", "As empty bins, so that any bin whose far edge passes the mute angle is left blank at that sample.", "As missing data, each replaced by the nearest live amplitude on the same offset trace before binning.", "As live samples, entering the bin mean like any other value."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A sample that is not finite or is a seismic null is skipped; the mute''s exact zeros are live samples, so a bin reaching past the mute angle averages reflection with zeros. That is why the mute angle and the bin edges must be read together.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 10, 'On gather 5 at 1292.000000 ms, with bin edges at 5, 15, 25 and 35 degrees, what fold and mean amplitude does the far bin, 25 to 35 degrees, carry?', '["Fold 3, mean amplitude 410.433207, the figures of the mid bin from 15 to 25 degrees at the same sample.", "Fold 2, mean amplitude 220.203720, the value of the kit''s own far stack trace at Ekene-1 at that time.", "Fold 2, mean amplitude 320.449005.", "Fold 13, every offset averaged."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The far bin holds 2 traces with mean amplitude 320.449005 in trace units. 410.433207 with fold 3 is the mid bin, 220.203720 is the kit''s far stack trace, and the gather''s 13 offsets do not all fall in 5 to 35 degrees at this time.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 11, 'What does partialStack return over a stated range of angle bins at a sample?', '["The sum of the bins'' mean amplitudes over the range, so a range of more bins always stacks to a larger amplitude.", "The fold-weighted mean of the bins, empty where no bin has data.", "The plain mean of the bins, fold ignored.", "The fold-weighted mean of the bins, with a zero written wherever no bin in the range carries any data."]'::jsonb, 1, 'partialStack is the fold-weighted mean of the bins at each sample, and it is empty where no bin has data. A sum would grow with the number of bins, a plain mean ignores fold, and writing zeros would make a gap look like a live amplitude.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 12, 'At 1292.000000 ms, what are the far partial stack from gather 5 and the kit''s far stack trace at Ekene-1?', '["320.449005 from gather 5 and 220.203720 on the kit''s trace.", "220.203720 from gather 5, 320.449005 on the kit''s trace.", "410.433207 and 357.560150, the 15 to 25 degree pair.", "483.072830 from gather 5 and 456.803711 on the kit''s trace, the near pair that correlates most closely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Over 25 to 35 degrees gather 5 gives 320.449005 and the kit''s far trace 220.203720. The 410.433207 and 357.560150 pair is the mid range and 483.072830 and 456.803711 the near range; over the window the near pair correlates at 0.997076 and the far pair at 0.961871.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 13, 'At the top of gather 5 the bins of 5 to 15, 15 to 25 and 25 to 35 degrees carry fold 2, 3 and 2. What does usableAngle return at a stated minimum fold of 3?', '["25 degrees, the far edge of the mid bin, the one bin whose fold of 3 meets the stated minimum.", "35.000000 degrees, because the three bins together carry far more than 3 traces at that sample.", "15 degrees, the far edge of the near bin, which is where the count from the nearest bin begins.", "None."]'::jsonb, 3, 'usableAngle counts from the nearest bin and returns the far edge of the last bin with at least the stated minimum fold, and none where even the first bin has too few. The near bin has fold 2, so a minimum of 3 gives none even though the mid bin has fold 3.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 14, 'A study states a minimum fold of 2 at the top of gather 5. What usable angle does usableAngle return?', '["40 degrees, the angle of the kit''s mute, which caps how far out in angle any bin can be trusted.", "49.493998 degrees, Walden''s angle at the gather''s farthest offset of 2500 m at the same time.", "35.000000 degrees.", "None, since the near bin''s fold of 2 sits exactly on the minimum and the engine needs more."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a minimum fold of 2 every bin from the nearest outwards qualifies (fold 2, 3, 2), so the usable angle is the far edge of the far bin, 35.000000 degrees; a fold equal to the minimum counts. The mute and the far-offset angle do not enter the rule.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-angles-from-velocity', 15, 'The opening row at 46.700000 ms has no row above it. What does the engine give as its interval velocity?', '["A refusal, since the first row has no shallower row to pair with.", "1500.000000 m/s, its RMS velocity, by the engine''s rule for the first row.", "2042.470033 m/s, the interval to the second row, carried upward.", "1974.900000 m/s, the second row''s RMS velocity, passed up a row."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first row''s interval velocity is its RMS velocity, 1500.000000 m/s. 2042.470033 is the interval from 46.700000 to 330.600000 ms and 1974.900000 is the second row''s RMS velocity; the engine does not refuse the first row.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 1, 'fitShuey runs with two terms on gather 5''s three angle bins at the top (1292.000000 ms), placed at 10, 20 and 30 degrees. What intercept and gradient come back?', '["A 510.227684 and B -915.097371, the three-term fit on the same three bins, its curvature carried in C.", "A 501.873676 and B -734.431814, in trace amplitude units.", "A 519.312395 and B -927.296460, from 9 bins of 5 degrees.", "A 486.838592 and B -1072.562182, from the kit''s stacks."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two-term fit on the near, mid and far bins at 10, 20 and 30 degrees gives A 501.873676 and B -734.431814. The three-term fit gives 510.227684 and -915.097371, nine 5 degree bins give 519.312395 and -927.296460, and 486.838592 with -1072.562182 is the fit to the kit''s stack traces at Ekene-1.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 2, 'fitShuey is asked for three terms on the three bins at gather 5''s top. What does the course say about that fit?', '["It is overdetermined, with one spare equation left to measure the misfit of the curvature term against the data.", "It is underdetermined, so the engine refuses a third term until a fourth angle carries a finite amplitude.", "It returns C as 0.000000, the same as the two-term fit, because three angles cannot resolve a curvature term.", "It is exactly determined: three terms on three angles."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A three-term fit on three angles is exactly determined. The engine returns C 467.947963 here; 0.000000 is the two-term fit''s C, which has no third term.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 3, 'Adding the third term at gather 5''s top changes the fit. What does the three-term fit return?', '["A 510.227684, B -915.097371 and C 467.947963.", "A 501.873676, B -734.431814 and C 467.947963.", "A 510.227684, B -734.431814 and C 0.000000.", "A 519.312395, B -927.296460 and C 0.000000."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The three-term fit gives A 510.227684, B -915.097371 and C 467.947963, against the two-term A 501.873676 and B -734.431814 on the same bins. 519.312395 and -927.296460 are the two-term fit on 9 bins.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 4, 'At one sample the far stack carries no finite amplitude. What does fitShuey do with that angle?', '["It reads the gap as an amplitude of zero, pulling the gradient steeply negative at that sample of the volume.", "It refuses the sample in the engine''s own words and leaves the volumes empty there.", "It drops it, fitting only the angles that carry a finite amplitude.", "It fills the gap with the mean of the near and mid amplitudes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'fitShuey fits per sample by least squares over the angles that carry a finite amplitude, so a gap drops out and the angles used can be fewer than the angles given. It neither zero-fills, refuses nor interpolates; a gap in a stack changes what the volumes rest on at that sample.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 5, 'The two-term intercept at gather 5''s top is 501.873676. In what units is that figure?', '["Reflection coefficient units, since the fit is Shuey''s approximation, which is written for reflectivity itself.", "Trace amplitude units, tied to reflectivity only by the one scale over the wells.", "Acoustic impedance in kg/(m2 s), the contrast across the top of the Ekene Sand.", "Degrees, the angle at which the fitted line crosses zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The kit''s seismic amplitudes carry no physical scale, so A and B from the stacks are in trace amplitude units. The course divides them by the one scale over the wells, 1882.216944, before reading them as reflection coefficients.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 6, 'What is an intercept volume, and where does QI Studio compute it?', '["The near stack relabelled, since the near angle sits closest to normal incidence across the whole survey.", "The Shuey fit at the top of each well only, the four well points then gridded across the survey by the app.", "The full stack scaled by 1882.216944, so that every sample of it reads as a reflection coefficient.", "The Shuey fit at every sample of every trace of the angle stacks, run on QI Studio''s seismic worker over the survey."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Intercept and gradient volumes are the same fit run at every sample of every trace of the angle stacks; QI Studio runs it on the seismic worker over the survey. The near stack is one input to the fit, and the one scale ties amplitudes to reflectivity after the fit.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 7, 'How does backgroundTrend fit the line through gather 5''s (A, B) pairs?', '["By ordinary least squares of B on A with a free intercept, the line allowed to miss the origin.", "By ordinary least squares of B on A through the origin.", "B = m A through the origin, by orthogonal regression over the pairs.", "By taking the published Castagna, Swan and Foster slope as it stands, with no fit to the seismic pairs at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The background trend is the line B = m A through the origin by orthogonal regression over the (A, B) pairs, 176 of them on gather 5 with slope -4.020851. The course names which line a distance is read from.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 8, 'On gather 5, the two-term fit at every sample of the window where all three bins carry data gives the (A, B) pairs. How many pairs, and what trend slope m do they give?', '["176 pairs, slope m -4.020851.", "13 pairs, one per offset, slope m -4.020851.", "176 pairs, slope m -76.033690, the slope given in degrees.", "176 pairs, slope m -1.16, the fluid factor''s coefficient on dVs/Vs."]'::jsonb, 0, '176 pairs give a background slope of -4.020851. -76.033690 is atan(m) in degrees, the chi along the trend, and 1.16 is the coefficient in the fluid factor; the gather''s 13 offsets are traces, and the pairs come from samples of the window.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 9, 'How far does the top''s point at gather 5 sit from that gather''s background trend?', '["833.848276, the projection of the point at chi = atan(m), read along the background trend.", "309.781098, in trace amplitude units.", "67.419446, the chi projection of the point at the stated chi of 30 degrees.", "0.107520, the fluid factor."]'::jsonb, 1, 'offTrend gives (B - m A) / sqrt(1 + m^2); the top''s two-term point sits 309.781098 from the line in trace amplitude units. 833.848276 and 67.419446 are chi projections and 0.107520 is the fluid factor of the scaled point.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 10, 'What does a negative off-trend distance mean?', '["The point sits above the line, towards harder responses than the background of the window.", "The point''s intercept is negative, whatever its gradient.", "The point sits on the line, in reverse polarity.", "The point sits below the line, towards softer, gas-like responses."]'::jsonb, 3, 'offTrend returns (B - m A) / sqrt(1 + m^2), negative towards softer, gas-like responses below the line; the course''s vocabulary calls negative the hydrocarbon side. The sign of the distance is a position against the line, which the intercept alone does not set.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 11, 'contrastsFromAB is run on the top of gather 5 after its two-term A and B are divided by 1882.216944, at a stated Vs/Vp of 0.500000. What fluid factor does it return?', '["0.426623, the relative compressional contrast dVp/Vp of the same point.", "0.550179, the relative shear contrast dVs/Vs at the stated Vs/Vp.", "0.107520.", "0.106656, the density contrast drho/rho from Gardner."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fluid factor dVp/Vp - 1.16 (Vs/Vp) dVs/Vs is 0.107520 here. 0.426623 is dVp/Vp, 0.550179 dVs/Vs and 0.106656 drho/rho, all from the A and B divided by the one scale at the stated Vs/Vp of 0.500000.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 12, 'How does contrastsFromAB tie the density contrast to the velocity contrasts?', '["By Gardner''s density, drho/rho = dVp/Vp / 4, which gives 0.106656 against dVp/Vp 0.426623 at the top.", "By the stated Vs/Vp of 0.500000, which sets the density contrast to half the shear contrast.", "By holding density fixed, so drho/rho reads zero and A carries only velocity.", "By 1.16 (Vs/Vp) dVs/Vs, the mudrock line term."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine uses Gardner''s density, drho/rho = dVp/Vp / 4, and the top gives drho/rho 0.106656 against dVp/Vp 0.426623. 1.16 (Vs/Vp) dVs/Vs is the term the fluid factor subtracts.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 13, 'A study passes the seismic A and B of a sample to contrastsFromAB straight from the stacks, in trace units. What does the course state about that input?', '["Trace units are fine, since the fluid factor is a ratio of contrasts and any common scale cancels out of it.", "The engine needs A and B as reflection coefficients, so they are first divided by the one scale.", "The engine rescales its inputs itself by the stated Vs/Vp, which is why that ratio must be given.", "The engine refuses any intercept above one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course states that the engine needs A and B as reflection coefficients. Seismic A and B sit in trace amplitude units, so a study divides them by the scale the comparison at the wells fits before the call.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 14, 'What does chiProjection return for the top of gather 5 at chi = atan(m) = -76.033690 degrees, the angle along the background trend?', '["67.419446, the projection of the same point at the stated chi of 30 degrees.", "309.781098, the off-trend distance of the point from the background line.", "-4.020851, the background trend slope m of gather 5.", "833.848276."]'::jsonb, 3, 'chiProjection is A cos(chi) + B sin(chi). At chi = atan(m), along the background trend, the top projects to 833.848276; at the stated 30 degrees it projects to 67.419446. 309.781098 is the off-trend distance and -4.020851 is m itself.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-avo-volumes', 15, 'QI Studio conditions its angle stacks before the Shuey fit. Which statement about that conditioning holds in this course?', '["QI Studio runs trim statics, matching in shift, phase and scale, and spectral balancing; the course vendors none of it, grades none of it, and a study records which steps it ran.", "The course''s prestack explorer runs all three steps on the pack''s stacks before every fit, so its A and B are conditioned.", "Conditioning moves the stacks in time only, so the amplitudes the fit reads are unchanged by it.", "The capstone grades the conditioned far stack."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Conditioning lives in the engines'' conditioning file and QI Studio runs it: trim statics, matching in shift, phase and scale, and spectral balancing. The course vendors and grades none of it, and each step changes the amplitudes the fit reads, so a study records which it ran.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 1, 'How does the comparison at the wells compute its one scale?', '["One scale per well, the well''s seismic A over its modelled A, then averaged over the four wells.", "From the gradients alone, since B is the larger number in both the modelled and the seismic columns.", "scale = sum(observed x modelled) / sum(modelled^2), over every well, A and B together.", "From the intercepts alone, sum(observed A) over sum(modelled A)."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One least-squares scale ties the seismic amplitudes to reflectivity over every well, A and B together: sum(observed x modelled) / sum(modelled^2). Over the four Ekene wells it is 1882.216944.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 2, 'What is the one scale over the four Ekene wells, and in what units?', '["1882.216944 trace units per unit reflection coefficient.", "1882.216944 reflection coefficient per trace unit, the factor applied the other way round.", "309.781098 trace units, the top''s off-trend distance.", "-4.020851, the gather 5 background slope."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Over the 4 wells the one scale is 1882.216944 trace units per unit reflection coefficient, so a seismic A or B is divided by it to read as reflectivity. 309.781098 is an off-trend distance in trace units and -4.020851 a slope with no units.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 3, 'Which two conditions does QI Studio raise from the comparison at the wells, and at what level?', '["A high issue for any residual above 0.088133, the Ekene-1 figure, and an issue for a well modelled over net sand.", "An issue for a tie whose mean windowed correlation is below 0.5, and a high one for a pick outside 8 ms.", "A high issue for a class that differs, none for the scale.", "An issue for a class that differs, a high one for a negative scale."]'::jsonb, 3, 'QI Studio raises an issue for a class that differs and a high one for a negative scale, a polarity or wavelet problem. The correlation below 0.5 is its rule for a poor tie, which belongs to the issue register for ties, and the course states no residual threshold.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 4, 'Ekene-2''s top of the Ekene Sand is at 5134.514436 ft. Why is its modelled intercept and gradient read from the cap rock over its net sand?', '["Its oil leg is thinner than the 65 ft cap rock, so the comparison widens the lower layer to the net sand.", "Its top lies below the oil-water contact at 5118.110236 ft, so it has no oil leg.", "Every well is modelled over its net sand, so the four wells share one rock and one scale fits them all.", "Its bulk shift of -4.000000 ms moves the top off the oil leg in time, so the net sand is read."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The modelled side is the cap rock over the oil leg, or over the net sand where a well has no oil leg; a well whose top lies below the contact at 5118.110236 ft has none, and Ekene-2''s oil leg count is 0. Ekene-1 and Ekene-3 are modelled over their oil legs.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 5, 'At Ekene-2 the checkshot time of the top is 1305.746335 ms. Where does the comparison read the seismic?', '["At 1304.000000 ms, the top picked on the near stack trace within 8 ms of its checkshot time.", "At 1305.746335 ms exactly, the checkshot time itself, with the near, mid and far stack traces read at that time.", "At 1292.000000 ms, the top picked on gather 5, the trace of Ekene-1.", "At the largest far stack amplitude within 20 ms, the bulk shift search."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The seismic top is picked on the near stack trace within 8 ms of the checkshot time, and the near, mid and far traces are read at that sample: 1304.000000 ms at Ekene-2. 1292.000000 ms is Ekene-1''s pick, and 20 ms is the bulk shift search.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 6, 'How is each well''s seismic A and B fitted in the comparison at the wells?', '["Three Shuey terms at 10, 20 and 30 degrees, the curvature term kept to absorb the far stack''s fall.", "Two Shuey terms on 9 bins of 5 degrees at their centres, the finer sampling of the gather.", "Two Shuey terms at the bin edges of 5, 15, 25 and 35 degrees on each angle stack trace.", "Two Shuey terms at 10, 20 and 30 degrees."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The near, mid and far stack traces are read at the picked sample and fitted with two Shuey terms at 10, 20 and 30 degrees. The three-term and 9 bin fits are the alternatives the Shuey lesson compared at gather 5.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 7, 'At Ekene-1 the comparison''s seismic A is 486.838592, while the two-term fit at gather 5''s top gave 501.873676. Which stacks does the comparison read?', '["The partial stacks of gather 5, the gather on the trace of Ekene-1, built from the NMO-corrected offsets.", "The full stack trace at the well, read at three angles from the RMS velocity table at the picked time.", "The near, mid and far stack traces at the well.", "Gather 5''s angle bins of 5 degrees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The seismic side reads the near, mid and far stack traces at the well at the picked sample, which give A 486.838592 at Ekene-1. 501.873676 is the fit to gather 5''s own partial stacks, a different stack of the same rock.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 8, 'What is a well''s residual in the comparison?', '["The off-trend distance of its seismic point from the background trend fitted to the gather''s pairs.", "The distance between its scaled seismic point and its modelled point on the (A, B) crossplot.", "The difference between its seismic and modelled intercepts, in trace units.", "The count of classes that differ."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each well''s seismic A and B are divided by the scale, and the residual is the distance between that scaled point and the modelled one, in reflection coefficient units. The off-trend distance is a different measure, from a line fitted to seismic pairs.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 9, 'Which Ekene well carries the largest residual against its model?', '["Ekene-1, at 0.088133, the well on which the gathers sit.", "Ekene-2, at 0.105803.", "Ekene-3, at 0.059452, the smallest of the four residuals.", "Ekene-4, at 0.077711."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-2''s scaled seismic point, A 0.265206 and B -0.410601, sits 0.105803 from its modelled point, above Ekene-1''s 0.088133, Ekene-4''s 0.077711 and Ekene-3''s 0.059452.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 10, 'What are Ekene-1''s seismic A and B after division by the one scale?', '["A 0.258652 and B -0.569840, against modelled A 0.170742 and B -0.563558.", "A 0.170742 and B -0.563558, the modelled point itself.", "A 486.838592 and B -1072.562182.", "A 0.249870 and B -0.663631, Ekene-3''s scaled point."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing 486.838592 and -1072.562182 by 1882.216944 gives the scaled seismic point A 0.258652, B -0.569840; the modelled point is 0.170742 and -0.563558, and the residual between them is 0.088133. 0.249870 and -0.663631 belong to Ekene-3.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 11, 'How do the modelled and seismic classes compare over the four Ekene wells?', '["The oil-leg wells Ekene-1 and Ekene-3 are class III on the seismic, so 2 of 4 agree with the model.", "Ekene-2, the largest residual, is class II on the seismic, so 3 of 4 agree with the model there.", "All four are class IV on both sides, since every gradient in the comparison is negative.", "4 of 4 agree, all class I."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every modelled and seismic point is class I at the band of 0.02, so 4 of 4 wells agree in class; the residuals range from 0.059452 to 0.105803.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 12, 'The comparison classes each point with avoClass. At what band, and is the class graded?', '["At a band of 0.5, the threshold below which a tie''s mean windowed correlation is called poor; graded.", "At a band of 0.8, the wavelet rule; the class agreement count is a graded figure.", "At the stated band of 0.02, and a class is never graded.", "At 0.02, and the count of wells that agree is graded."]'::jsonb, 2, 'avoClass runs at the band 0.02. The course never grades any verdict, class name or category; 0.5 and 0.8 are QI Studio''s rules for a poor tie and for a wavelet unlike the others.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 13, 'What does the Professional capstone''s case file hold?', '["The Ekene pack''s gather 5 and its RMS table, with the logs of Ekene-1 to Ekene-4 for the learner to model.", "One NMO-corrected CDP gather with its RMS velocity table, and modelled and seismic intercepts and gradients at four wells.", "The logs of four wells and their checkshots, from which the learner builds the ties and the angle stacks.", "Four wells'' angle stack traces only, with the scale."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The capstone runs on a held-out synthetic case: one NMO-corrected CDP gather with its RMS velocity table, and modelled and seismic intercepts and gradients at four wells, pasted into the prestack explorer. Every input the figures depend on is stated in the case file.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 14, 'Why is the Professional capstone never run on the Ekene field?', '["The Ekene field is synthetic, and graded figures must come from a real survey with real wells and logs.", "The Ekene pack''s gathers carry the kit''s mute beyond 40 degrees, which no capstone can work around.", "The pack''s logs give too few live synthetic samples for a well wavelet, so the tie cannot be graded.", "The members-only videos speak the Ekene answers aloud, so a graded Ekene figure could be answered by watching."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The capstone cases are never the Ekene field because the members-only videos speak the Ekene answers aloud. The capstone cases are themselves held-out synthetic cases, so a synthetic field is no bar.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-avo-at-the-wells', 15, 'Which of these is one of the six figures the Professional capstone grades?', '["The one scale over the four wells.", "The largest residual among the four wells.", "The number of wells whose classes agree.", "The background trend slope m of the gather."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The six graded figures are the Dix interval velocity of a stated layer, Walden''s angle, the NMO stretch, the far partial stack amplitude, the two-term Shuey intercept and the one scale over the four wells. No class or count is graded, and the residual and the trend slope are not among the six.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'Ekene-1''s synthetic correlates 0.983438 with its full-stack trace at zero shift. Which later result at Ekene-1 prints that same figure?', '["The phase match''s correlation after rotation, at its best phase of -6.135261 degrees.", "Ekene-1''s correlation with the field wavelet in the misfit table of the four wells.", "The bulk shift''s correlation, found at a shift of 0.000000 ms.", "The 60 degree row of the designed phase case."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-1''s bulk shift is 0.000000 ms at a correlation of 0.983438, the figure the zero-shift synthetic and the phase match''s reading before rotation also print. After rotation the phase match reads 0.989111, the misfit table gives Ekene-1 0.999838, and the 60 degree row reads 0.988816 after matching.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'suggestBulkShift returns -4.000000 ms at Ekene-2. Which engine file does that function come from?', '["engines/seismolord/synthetics.js, the file that also builds the synthetic and the statistical wavelet.", "engines/seismolord/tieWarp.js, beside makeTieWarp, since a single anchor is a bulk shift.", "engines/qi/wavelets.js, beside compareWavelets, which aligns by the lag of largest correlation.", "engines/qi/prestackQc.js, beside residualMoveout, which finds each offset''s lag against a pilot."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tier''s table lists suggestBulkShift in engines/seismolord/synthetics.js with computeImpedance, reflectivity, convolveSame and extractStatisticalWavelet; tieWarp.js holds the phase functions and makeTieWarp, wavelets.js holds compareWavelets, and prestackQc.js holds the gather QC. Ekene-4 also ties at -4.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'Which statement about the statistical wavelet that extractStatisticalWavelet builds is true?', '["Its phase is measured, as the least-squares wavelet from the well''s reflectivity.", "It needs a log long enough for the wavelet asked for.", "It is scaled to unit energy and aligned by up to 5 samples.", "Its spectrum is the source wavelet''s when the reflectivity is white, and its phase is assumed zero."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The statistical wavelet is the amplitude spectrum of the traces'' autocorrelation taken to zero phase, so its phase is assumed, and every one of the four has 31 samples on the 4.000000 ms clock. A measured phase and the need for a long log belong to the well wavelet; unit energy and alignment by up to 5 samples are steps of compareWavelets.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'A 25 Hz Ricker on the same 4.000000 ms clock is set beside the four statistical wavelets. What centroid does it carry?', '["25.039790 Hz, the same as its peak, since a Ricker''s power sits evenly about its peak.", "26.596139 Hz, above every statistical wavelet''s centroid, the highest of which is Ekene-4''s 26.581599 Hz.", "25.155328 Hz, the centroid of the field wavelet averaged from the four wells.", "26.530951 Hz, below Ekene-4''s own centroid, since a Ricker chosen by its peak carries less power at high frequency."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the same clock the Ricker''s peak is 25.039790 Hz and its centroid 26.596139 Hz, while the wells'' centroids run from 23.431642 Hz at Ekene-2 to 26.581599 Hz at Ekene-4. 25.155328 Hz is the average wavelet''s centroid and 26.530951 Hz is Ekene-4''s peak.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'In which units does the Professional tier read its depths, offsets, times and velocities?', '["Depth in feet, offsets in metres as the kit''s gathers record them, times in milliseconds two-way, velocities in m/s.", "Depth and offsets both in feet, the offsets converted from the kit''s metres, velocities in m/s.", "Depth in feet, offsets in metres, times one-way as checkshots record them, velocities in m/s.", "Depth in feet, offsets in metres, times two-way, and seismic amplitudes as reflection coefficients with a physical scale."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tier keeps depth in feet, offsets in metres, two-way times in milliseconds and velocities in m/s; seismic amplitudes stay in the kit''s trace units, which carry no physical scale until the one scale of 1882.216944 ties them to reflectivity. A checkshot records one-way time, and the time-depth line takes two-way time as 2 x the one-way time.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'A study reads a stretch mute offset of 2047.359898 m at the top of gather 5. Which function and engine file return it?', '["binOffsets, in engines/qi/prestack.js, which places each offset in a bin of stated width.", "nmoStretch, in the synthetics.js file.", "stretchMuteOffset, in engines/qi/prestackQc.js.", "usableAngle, in engines/qi/prestack.js."]'::jsonb, 2, 'prestackQc.js holds nmoStretch, stretchMuteOffset, residualMoveout and foldSummary; stretchMuteOffset returns v t0 sqrt((1 + limit)^2 - 1), 2047.359898 m at a limit of 0.200000. binOffsets and usableAngle sit in prestack.js, and synthetics.js holds the synthetic, the statistical wavelet and the bulk shift.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'The field wavelet compareWavelets builds at Ekene has 31 samples. What are its members, and why is the well wavelet absent?', '["Four well wavelets with measured phase, since every well ties at 0.962720 or better.", "The four statistical wavelets, since the pack''s logs cover only the Ekene Sand and the shale above it.", "The four statistical wavelets, after a well wavelet''s 31 samples are resampled to 61.", "Three well wavelets and Ekene-2''s statistical one, since Ekene-2 alone needs a bulk shift of -4.000000 ms to tie."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At its default 31 samples the well wavelet on Ekene-1''s 26 live samples returns the engine''s message that the reflectivity is flat; the pack''s logs cover the Ekene Sand and the shale above it only, so the course''s ties use the statistical wavelet, and the average keeps its members'' 31 samples. 61 is the average resampled from 4 ms to 2 ms, and Ekene-4 also shifts by -4.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'At the top on gather 5, the three angle bins read 483.072830, 410.433207 and 320.449005. What does the sign of all three agree with?', '["Reversed polarity, where an increase in impedance downwards is a trough on the kit''s seismic and the top of the sand is softer.", "The synthetic''s coefficient of -0.190340 at 1312.000000 ms, the largest in its span, which dominates every bin at the top.", "The mute''s exact zeros, which enter every bin''s mean and lift it above zero.", "SEG normal polarity: the Ekene Sand is harder than the Ogbia Shale above it at Ekene-1, so the top is a peak."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The kit''s seismic is SEG normal polarity, an increase in impedance downwards is a peak, a positive number, and the sand is harder than the shale above it at Ekene-1, so its top reads positive in every bin. The -0.190340 coefficient sits at 1312.000000 ms, and zeros could only pull a mean towards zero.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'Of the four statistical wavelets, which pair does compareWavelets find least alike, and where do those two sit in frequency?', '["Ekene-2 with Ekene-4 at 0.986771, the wells with the lowest and highest peaks, 23.394907 and 26.530951 Hz.", "Ekene-1 with Ekene-3 at 0.999524, the two wells whose peaks lie closest together in frequency.", "Ekene-1 with Ekene-4 at 0.996130, since Ekene-1 is the reference every other wavelet is aligned to.", "Ekene-2 with Ekene-3 at 0.994059, the pair with the largest lag between them after the alignment."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The similarity is the zero-lag correlation of aligned, unit-energy wavelets; the lowest off-diagonal figure is Ekene-2 with Ekene-4, 0.986771, and their peaks, 23.394907 and 26.530951 Hz, are the lowest and highest of the four. 0.999524 is the most alike pair, and every lag to the reference is 0.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'The comparison at the wells models Ekene-3 from its oil leg and Ekene-4 from net sand. What do the course''s selections count in those two wells'' oil legs?', '["65 at Ekene-3, 135 at Ekene-4.", "40 at Ekene-3 and 0 at Ekene-4.", "114 samples at Ekene-3 and 0 at Ekene-4.", "154 at Ekene-3 and 0 at Ekene-4."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The selections count Ekene-3 at 154 net sand, 114 oil leg and 40 water leg, and Ekene-4 at 135 net sand, 0 oil leg and 135 water leg, so the comparison reads Ekene-3''s cap rock over its oil leg and Ekene-4''s over its net sand. 65 belongs to Ekene-1.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, '4 of 4 Ekene wells agree in class at the stated band of 0.02. What status does a class have in the course''s grading?', '["It is graded as the sixth capstone figure, since the comparison at the wells returns it beside the one scale.", "It is never graded, like any verdict, class name or category.", "It is graded only where the modelled and seismic classes differ, because QI Studio raises an issue there.", "It is graded on the Ekene wells alone, where 4 of 4 agree, since a class needs the real survey behind it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The capstone grades six numeric return values on a held-out synthetic case that is not the Ekene field; no verdict, class name or category is graded. The sixth figure is the one scale over the four wells, which at Ekene is 1882.216944.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Rank the four ties by bulk-shift correlation. Which well comes last, and what shift does its tie carry?', '["Ekene-2, 0.965477, at -4.000000 ms.", "Ekene-3, 0.977607, at 0.000000 ms.", "Ekene-1, 0.983438, at 0.000000 ms.", "Ekene-4, 0.962720, at -4.000000 ms."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ranked by correlation, Ekene-1 reads 0.983438, Ekene-3 0.977607, Ekene-2 0.965477 and Ekene-4 0.962720; the two lowest are the two wells whose ties move by -4.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Ekene-4''s top of the Ekene Sand lies at 5216.535433 ft. What two-way time does the time-depth line give it, and where does the comparison at the wells pick it on the near stack?', '["1305.746335 ms by the checkshots, picked at 1304.000000 ms.", "1329.290304 ms by the checkshots, picked at 1328.000000 ms.", "1282.159191 ms by the checkshots, picked at 1284.000000 ms.", "1288.796378 ms, picked at 1292.000000 ms, one time serving every well of the field."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Linear between checkshot levels, Ekene-4''s top is at 1329.290304 ms, the latest of the four, and the near-stack pick within 8 ms of it lands on 1328.000000 ms. The other pairs are Ekene-2''s, Ekene-3''s and Ekene-1''s.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Ekene-1''s phase match finds a best phase of -6.135261 degrees. Under QI Studio''s issue register, when are wavelet phases across wells flagged?', '["When they span more than 30 degrees across the wells.", "When any one well''s best phase is other than zero, the phase a statistical wavelet assumes.", "When a well''s wavelet falls below 0.8.", "When a tie''s mean windowed correlation falls below 0.7, the register''s fair line."]'::jsonb, 0, 'QI Studio flags wavelet phases across wells that span more than 30 degrees; the 0.8 rule marks a well whose wavelet is unlike the others, and 0.7 and 0.5 grade a tie''s correlation as fair and poor. A phase of -6.135261 degrees at one well is a figure its tie states.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'Two wavelets on different sample intervals must be brought to one before averaging. What does resampleWavelet do, and how many samples does the Ekene average carry on a 2 ms clock?', '["It keeps the 31 samples and halves the interval, so the wavelet is squeezed into half the time.", "It refuses wavelets of unequal length, as averageSpectrum refuses traces of 16 and 20 samples.", "It interpolates linearly about the centre sample, and the average carries 61 samples.", "It rebuilds each wavelet from its spectrum at the new interval."]'::jsonb, 2, 'resampleWavelet brings wavelets to one interval by linear interpolation about the centre sample, and the Ekene average resampled from 4 ms to 2 ms has 61 samples against its 31. The refusal of traces of 16 and 20 samples belongs to averageSpectrum.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'In the designed case, Ekene-3''s wavelet is rotated by 90 degrees before the comparison. What does it score against the average, and what does QI Studio''s 0.8 rule do with it?', '["0.693702, below 0.8, so the well is flagged as unlike the others and is left out of the field wavelet.", "0.999751, its unrotated figure.", "0.988309, the phase match''s figure after rotation.", "0.911984, above 0.8, so the similarity rule passes it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Rotated by 90 degrees, Ekene-3 correlates 0.911984 with the average and stays above the 0.8 rule; reversed by 180 degrees it falls to 0.693702, below it. 0.999751 is its unrotated figure and 0.988309 belongs to the phase match on the synthetic, a different engine call.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'With Ekene-3''s wavelet reversed in polarity, what happens to Ekene-1''s correlation with the average?', '["It stays at 0.999838, since only the reversed well''s own figure moves when one wavelet in the comparison is rotated.", "It falls from 0.999838 to 0.971738, as the average Ekene-3 pulled lowers the other wells'' figures too.", "It falls to 0.693702, the figure Ekene-3 itself scores.", "It falls to 0.990887, its figure with Ekene-3 at 90 degrees."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With Ekene-3 reversed, Ekene-1 reads 0.971738 against its unrotated 0.999838, because the average Ekene-3 pulled lowers the other three wells'' figures as well. 0.693702 is Ekene-3''s own figure and 0.990887 is Ekene-1''s with Ekene-3 rotated by 90 degrees.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'Two picks of the top are made near Ekene-1: on the nearest offset of gather 5, and on the kit''s near stack trace for the comparison at the wells. Where do they land?', '["Both on 1292.000000 ms.", "1292.000000 ms on the gather, and 1288.796378 ms on the near stack, the checkshot time read with no pick.", "1288.800000 ms on both, the RMS table''s row nearest the top, which the picks snap to.", "1292.000000 ms on the gather, and 1284.000000 ms on the near stack, two samples earlier."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gather 5''s pick about 1289 ms lands on 1292.000000 ms, and the comparison''s pick within 8 ms of the checkshot time 1288.796378 ms lands on 1292.000000 ms too; each takes the sample of largest amplitude near a stated time. 1284.000000 ms is Ekene-3''s pick.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'Which stated settings does residualMoveout run with on gather 5 about the top?', '["A pilot of all 13 traces, a half window of 8 ms as in the pick, and a largest shift of 20 ms as in the bulk shift search.", "A pilot of 5 traces, the comparison''s lag, and a half window of 120 ms, the statistical wavelet''s window length.", "A pilot of 3 traces, a half window of 24 ms and a largest shift of 40 ms.", "A pilot of the 3 nearest traces, a half window of 40 ms and a largest shift of 24 ms."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pilot stacks the stated 3 nearest traces, and each offset''s lag is the largest absolute correlation in a half window of 40 ms with a largest shift of 24 ms; offsets at least 0.7 in size enter the fit. 8 ms is the pick window, 20 ms the bulk shift search, 5 samples the wavelet comparison''s lag and 120 ms the wavelet window.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'The farthest offset of gather 5, 2500 m, carries the largest stretch at the top, 0.286880. What incidence angle does it reach there by Walden''s equation?', '["45.265069 degrees, the teaching earth''s figure.", "49.493998 degrees, beyond the far bin''s 25 to 35 degrees.", "44.057814 degrees, the exact ray at 2500 m, which the Ekene table follows by Snell''s law.", "35.000000 degrees, the usable angle."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the Ekene table at 1292.000000 ms Walden''s angle at 2500 m is 49.493998 degrees, past the 25 to 35 degree far bin. 45.265069 and 44.057814 degrees are the teaching earth''s Walden and ray-traced angles at 2500 m, and 35.000000 degrees is the usable angle at a minimum fold of 2.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'On gather 5 the far-offset residual moveout is 2.795940 ms, read as a x_far^2. At what far offset, and with how many offsets in the fit?', '["2047.359898 m, the stretch mute at 0.200000, with only the offsets inside it fitted.", "2563.848741 m, with 13 offsets fitted.", "2500.000000 m, with all 13 offsets fitted.", "2500.000000 m, with the 3 pilot traces."]'::jsonb, 2, 'residualMoveout on gather 5 uses 13 offsets, fits 13 and reports a far offset of 2500.000000 m with a residual of 2.795940 ms. The two stretch mute offsets, 2047.359898 and 2563.848741 m, come from stretchMuteOffset, and the 3 pilot traces build the pilot the lags are measured against.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'Mapped over inlines 1026 to 1028, where does the far-offset residual moveout reach its smallest value?', '["Gather 5, inline 1027, crossline 2051, the trace of Ekene-1, at 2.795940 ms.", "Gather 3, inline 1026, crossline 2053, at 3.002917 ms, a corner of the map.", "Gather 1, inline 1026, crossline 2051, at 2.819016 ms, on Ekene-1''s crossline.", "Gather 8, inline 1028, crossline 2050, at 2.824094 ms, measured by the lag fit."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The residuals run from 2.795940 ms at gather 5, the gather on Ekene-1''s trace, to 3.002917 ms at gather 3, and every gather is measured by ab-semblance with 13 offsets fitted; gathers 1 and 8 read 2.819016 and 2.824094 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'A study tightens usableAngle''s minimum fold one step at a time from 1 at the top of gather 5, where the Shuey fit reads bins holding 2, 3 and 2 traces. At which minimum does the usable range first vanish?', '["4, one above the mid bin''s fold of 3, the largest of the three.", "2, the course''s stated working minimum, which the 25 to 35 degree bin only just meets.", "None does, since the mid bin''s fold of 3 qualifies at any minimum up to 3.", "3, because counting starts at the 5 to 15 degree bin, whose fold is 2."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The count runs outwards from the near bin and stops at the first that falls short, so the near bin''s 2 traces decide: minimums of 1 and 2 reach 35.000000 degrees, and 3 leaves nothing, whatever the mid bin holds.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'The stretch at the top on gather 5 is read with an NMO velocity of 2388.940541 m/s. Where does that figure come from?', '["The Dix interval velocity of the segment from 1288.800000 to 1311.000000 ms, the velocity of the sand itself.", "The RMS row at 1288.800000 ms, 2387.600000 m/s, the row nearest the top, read with no interpolation.", "The RMS velocity of the table on the trace clock at 1292.000000 ms, linear in time between the rows that bracket it.", "Walden''s ratio Vint / Vrms applied to the row at 1311.000000 ms, 2396.900000 m/s, to reach the zero-offset time."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a trace the RMS velocity is linear in time between rows and held flat outside the table; at 1292.000000 ms it is 2388.940541 m/s, and the stretch takes the table''s RMS velocity there as its NMO velocity. The interval velocity at that time, 2885.878392 m/s, is what Walden''s angle reads beside it.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'Two mutes come up for the gathers next to Ekene-1: the kit''s stated mute and the stretch mute. Which statement holds?', '["Both are offsets a study states, 2047.359898 m for the kit and 2563.848741 m for the stretch.", "The kit''s mute is an angle, 40 degrees, from the kit''s own calculation; the stretch mute is an offset that follows from a stated limit.", "The course measures the kit''s mute with Walden''s angle, which passes 40 degrees at 2000 m.", "The stretch mute is fixed by the kit at 2563.848741 m, and the kit''s mute follows it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'stretchMuteOffset gives 2047.359898 m at a limit of 0.200000 and 2563.848741 m at 0.300000, so the stretch mute moves with the study''s choice; the 40 degree mute arrives in the pack already applied, by an angle calculation the course does not measure.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'Which interval of the pack''s RMS velocity table carries the fastest Dix interval velocity?', '["1288.800000 to 1311.000000 ms, at 2885.878392 m/s.", "330.600000 to 925.500000 ms, at 2689.639308 m/s, the interval above the row at 925.500000 ms.", "1571.700000 to 1624.800000 ms, 2523.323799 m/s.", "The first row, 1500.000000 m/s."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dix gives Vint^2 = (Vrms2^2 t2 - Vrms1^2 t1) / (t2 - t1); the largest interval velocity on the table is 2885.878392 m/s, over 1288.800000 to 1311.000000 ms, above 2689.639308 and 2523.323799 m/s, and the first row''s interval velocity is its RMS velocity, 1500.000000 m/s.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'Which of the course''s three angle bins does the 1000 m offset fall in at the top on gather 5, and at what straight-ray angle?', '["19.716435 degrees, Walden''s angle at 1000 m on the teaching earth, in the mid bin.", "11.138104 degrees, in the 5 to 15 degree near bin.", "31.872141 degrees, in the 25 to 35 degree far bin.", "21.859471 degrees, in the 15 to 25 degree mid bin."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 1000 m Walden''s equation gives 21.859471 degrees, inside 15 to 25 degrees; on the same table 500 m gives 11.138104 and 1500 m 31.872141 degrees, and the teaching earth gives 19.716435 at 1000 m.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'At the base of the third layer of the designed teaching earth, which zero-offset time and velocities does Walden''s angle read?', '["t0 1292.000000 ms, Vrms 2388.940541 m/s and Vint 2885.878392 m/s, the Ekene top.", "t0 1596.736597 ms, Vrms 2600 m/s and Vint 2152.777869 m/s.", "t0 1596.736597 ms, Vrms 2152.777869 m/s and Vint 2600 m/s.", "t0 1596.736597 ms, Vrms 2152.777869 m/s and Vint 3000 m/s, the fourth layer''s velocity."]'::jsonb, 2, 'layeredRms gives the third layer''s base at t0 1596.736597 ms with Vrms 2152.777869 m/s, and the layer itself runs at 2600 m/s; 3000 m/s is the fourth layer, and 1292.000000 ms with 2388.940541 and 2885.878392 m/s belongs to the Ekene table.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'At Ekene-1 the comparison''s seismic gradient is -1072.562182. What gradient did the two-term fit on gather 5''s own angle bins give at the same time?', '["-915.097371, the B of the three-term fit on the same three bins.", "-734.431814.", "-927.296460, the nine-bin B.", "-0.563558, Ekene-1''s modelled gradient."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Gather 5''s bins at 10, 20 and 30 degrees give a two-term B of -734.431814 at 1292.000000 ms; the comparison fits the kit''s near, mid and far stack traces, whose far trace reads 220.203720 against the gather''s 320.449005, and returns -1072.562182. -0.563558 is a reflection coefficient from the logs.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'The comparison at the wells fits its seismic A and B with two Shuey terms at 10, 20 and 30 degrees. What are those three angles?', '["The mean angles of the 5 to 15, 15 to 25 and 25 to 35 degree bins, the ranges the kit''s near, mid and far stacks span.", "Walden''s angles at 500, 1000 and 1500 m on the Ekene table at the top of gather 5.", "The usable angle of 35.000000 degrees split into three equal steps from the near offset.", "The ray-traced angles at the bases of the teaching earth''s three upper layers."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fit places the three bins at their mean angles of 10, 20 and 30 degrees, and the kit built its near, mid and far stacks over 5 to 15, 15 to 25 and 25 to 35 degrees; Walden''s angles at 500, 1000 and 1500 m on the Ekene table are 11.138104, 21.859471 and 31.872141 degrees.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'The two-term fit at gather 5''s top is rerun on 9 bins of 5 degrees at their centres in place of three. What comes back?', '["A 510.227684 and B -915.097371, the figures of the three-term fit on the three bins.", "A 501.873676 and B -734.431814 again, since narrower bins add no new traces to the fit.", "An exactly determined fit with C 467.947963, since 9 angles fix three terms.", "A 519.312395 and B -927.296460, from 9 angles."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On 9 bins of 5 degrees the two-term fit returns A 519.312395 and B -927.296460 with 9 angles used, both larger in size than the three-bin A 501.873676 and B -734.431814. The three-term fit on three angles, exactly determined, gives A 510.227684, B -915.097371 and C 467.947963.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'Against gather 5''s background trend, slope -4.020851 over 176 pairs, the top''s point sits 309.781098 from the line. Which side, and what does that side mean?', '["The negative side, below the line, on the softer, gas-like side where a hydrocarbon at Ekene-1 would put it.", "The positive side, above the line, away from the softer, gas-like responses that offTrend counts negative.", "The positive side, which offTrend counts towards the softer, gas-like responses lying below the trend line.", "Neither: a fitted point sits on the line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'offTrend is (B - m A) / sqrt(1 + m^2), negative towards softer, gas-like responses below the line; the top''s point, A 501.873676 and B -734.431814, reads 309.781098, positive, above it. A point inside the fitted pairs can still sit off an orthogonal regression line.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'At a stated Vs/Vp of 0.500000, contrastsFromAB turns the top''s scaled A and B into dVp/Vp 0.426623, dVs/Vs 0.550179 and drho/rho 0.106656. What sets drho/rho?', '["Gardner''s density, drho/rho = dVp/Vp / 4, applied to the dVp/Vp of 0.426623.", "The stated Vs/Vp of 0.500000 applied to dVs/Vs, so drho/rho follows the shear contrast of 0.550179.", "The fluid factor, dVp/Vp - 1.16 (Vs/Vp) dVs/Vs, which returns 0.107520 and fixes the density contrast with it.", "The cap rock''s density log over the oil leg''s."]'::jsonb, 0, 'contrastsFromAB takes A and B, a stated Vs/Vp and Gardner''s density, drho/rho = dVp/Vp / 4, so 0.426623 gives 0.106656. The fluid factor, 0.107520, is computed from the contrasts; it does not set them.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'Which references does the course name for the fluid factor and for the chi projection?', '["Castagna and Swan (1997) for the fluid factor, Shuey (1985) for chi.", "Whitcombe and others (2002) for the fluid factor, Smith and Gidlow (1987) for chi.", "Smith and Gidlow (1987) for the fluid factor, Whitcombe and others (2002) for chi.", "Walden (1991) for the fluid factor, Yilmaz (2001) for chi."]'::jsonb, 2, 'contrastsFromAB follows Smith and Gidlow (1987) and chiProjection Whitcombe and others (2002); Castagna and Swan (1997) is the background trend, Shuey (1985) the intercept and gradient, Walden (1991) the straight-ray angle and Yilmaz (2001) the NMO stretch.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'A study maps A cos(chi) + B sin(chi) at a stated chi of 30 degrees. What does the top''s point, A 501.873676 and B -734.431814, project to?', '["833.848276, the projection at chi = atan(m), along the background trend.", "501.873676, the intercept itself, which any chi projection returns unchanged.", "309.781098, the off-trend distance, which a chi of 30 degrees reads across the trend.", "67.419446."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A chi of 30 degrees weights the intercept by its cosine and the gradient by its sine, giving 67.419446; at -76.033690 degrees, along the background trend, the same point gives 833.848276. offTrend measures 309.781098 across that line, and a chi of zero returns A itself.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Which of these is one of the three steps QI Studio runs to condition angle stacks before the AVO fit?', '["AB semblance, the measure residualMoveout changes to when its lags scatter.", "Spectral balancing.", "The stretch mute, applied at a stated limit such as 0.200000.", "The Dix conversion of the RMS velocity table, turning each stack''s times into interval velocities."]'::jsonb, 1, 'QI Studio''s conditioning is trim statics between the stacks, matching in shift, phase and scale, and spectral balancing, in engines/qi/conditioning.js, which the course vendors none of. AB semblance and the stretch mute at 0.200000 belong to the gather QC, and the Dix conversion to angles from velocity.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'contrastsFromAB needs A and B as reflection coefficients. How does the course get them from gather 5''s top, A 501.873676 and B -734.431814?', '["It divides both by the one scale over the four wells, 1882.216944, fitted by the comparison at the wells.", "It divides both by the trend slope of -4.020851, the ratio of B to A that the background trend fits over 176 pairs.", "It reads them as they stand from the Shuey fit.", "It takes Ekene-1''s modelled A and B, 0.170742 and -0.563558."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fluid factor needs A and B as reflection coefficients, and the course divides the top''s two-term A and B by the one scale of 1882.216944, then reads dVp/Vp 0.426623 and a fluid factor of 0.107520 at Vs/Vp 0.500000. The fit''s A and B are in trace amplitude units.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'The top''s off-trend distance on gather 5 is 309.781098. In what units is it?', '["Reflection coefficient units already, since the background trend is fitted to modelled interfaces taken to brine.", "Degrees, the angle off the trend line.", "Milliseconds, the point''s residual off the trend in time, as residualMoveout reports at the far offset.", "In the kit''s trace amplitude units, the units of the A and B the trend was fitted to."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The background trend is fitted to the seismic (A, B) pairs of gather 5, so the top''s 309.781098 is in trace amplitude units; seismic amplitudes become reflection coefficients only through the one least-squares scale over the wells, 1882.216944.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'After division by the one scale, which well''s seismic gradient is the least negative?', '["Ekene-4, -0.475262, against a modelled -0.520090.", "Ekene-1, -0.569840, against a modelled -0.563558.", "Ekene-2, -0.410601, against a modelled -0.501240.", "Ekene-3, -0.663631, against a modelled -0.641524."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The scaled seismic gradients are -0.569840 at Ekene-1, -0.410601 at Ekene-2, -0.663631 at Ekene-3 and -0.475262 at Ekene-4, so Ekene-2 is the least negative, against its modelled -0.501240 from net sand.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'Ekene-3''s scaled seismic point is A 0.249870 and B -0.663631. Which modelled point does the comparison at the wells set it against?', '["A 0.216991 and B -0.520090, from the cap rock over its net sand, the layer Ekene-4 is modelled from.", "A 0.194681 and B -0.641524, from the cap rock over its oil leg.", "A 470.308887 and B -1249.098330, its own seismic point before the division by the one scale of 1882.216944.", "A 0.170742 and B -0.563558, from Ekene-1''s oil leg, the well whose trace gather 5 carries."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-3 holds 114 oil-leg samples, so its modelled side is the cap rock over its oil leg, A 0.194681 and B -0.641524, at a residual of 0.059452 from the scaled seismic point. 470.308887 and -1249.098330 are its seismic A and B before the division, and the other two points belong to Ekene-4 and Ekene-1.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'The kit''s seismic is SEG normal polarity, and the one scale at Ekene is positive. Which result of the comparison would QI Studio rate as a high issue?', '["A negative scale, the sign of a polarity or wavelet problem.", "A class that differs at one well.", "A positive scale, read as reversed seismic.", "A wavelet correlation of 0.693702 with the average, read by the comparison at the wells."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At Ekene the one scale is 1882.216944, positive, and 4 of 4 wells agree in class; QI Studio keeps its high rating for a negative scale, which points to polarity or the wavelet. 0.693702 is Ekene-3''s reversed wavelet in the tie register.'),
  ('qi', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'In the Professional capstone, the NMO stretch is read at the same offset and time as which other graded figure?', '["The Dix interval velocity.", "The two-term Shuey intercept.", "The one scale over the four wells.", "Walden''s angle at a stated offset and time."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The case states the offset and time, and Walden''s angle and the stretch both read them: at Ekene, at 2500 m and 1292.000000 ms, Walden gives 49.493998 degrees and the stretch is 0.286880. The Dix velocity reads two rows of the table, the intercept reads the target sample, and the scale reads the four wells.');
